  "title": "Resting ants: does either colony read as alive rather than stuck?",
  "question": "Scrub both. In which one do the animals that are standing still look like they are resting — shifting, settling, moving on — rather than stuck?",
  "context_md": "**The complaint this is about.** On round 29's card you marked three long ants *\"no movement\"*. They were resting, and the counters agreed — so it was filed as a look problem. Your reply overturned that: *\"If they never ask to move that is still stuck, it is just because the rest mechanism needs fixing.\"* This is the rest mechanism fixed, put back in front of you.\n\n**What was actually wrong.** An ant's urge to walk is a sum of reasons, squashed into a probability. When the reasons came out negative — fed, food within reach, neighbours close — the probability was clipped to **exactly zero**, and from there no dice roll can ever produce a step. Nothing the animal does gets it out; only the world changing around it does. Measured over 120,000-frame beds: that exact zero is where **49–64%** of a long ant's decisions landed, and **54–78%** of a shipped two-cell ant's. The longest single stand was **33,300 frames**. *The shipped ant rests as long as the long one* — this was never a long-ant problem, it is every animal in both games.\n\n**The change.** Standing still is now itself a reason to move, growing slowly the longer an animal holds one cell. So a rest **ends**, the animal shifts, and it settles again. The typical short pause is deliberately untouched — median rest is 4 ticks in both arms below — only the tail that reads as stuck is cut.\n\n**What I am unsure of, and would rather you told me than that I guessed.** Whether this reads as *resting animals* or as *animals that never settle*. Both are wrong; the target is in between. The 'standing right now' line under each pane is the sharpest number: it is how long the stillest animal on screen has held one cell, at the last frame.\n\n**Same seed, same window, same crop and zoom as the card you marked.** The two are different worlds by this frame — a change this deep re-rolls the whole run — so read the colonies, not cell-for-cell. 80 seconds of play, one frame every 2 seconds. Rain off. The 'PAUSED' in the corner is a harness artifact; the box is running.",
